{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790481932,"nonce":"QSEf1Q6W7ArtcvMzdSfF6RzIHTQ=","title":"Compiler: list lengths reloaded inside counted loops leave bounds versioning only sign checks","message":"Bounds versioning (bounds_version.rs) decides a length check only when\nthe length is an SSA value defined before the loop, i.e. when LICM has\nhoisted the header load. In most hot counted loops of the speed kernels\nthe load stays in the loop, so the copy could decide only the sign\nchecks and the loop is left as it is (\"no bound decided, only signs\"\nunder ZYNTAX_TRACE_BOUNDS_VERSION=1): nbody_modified advance, float\nbenchmark/maximize, spectral-norm part_A_times_u / part_At_times_u,\nscimark LU_factor, ArrayList.copy_data_from, FFT_inverse's second loop.\n\nOne concrete cause, seen on `for i in range(n): xs[i] += 1` over\nlist[int]: the inlined store's failing arm calls zb_fatal and then\nrejoins the loop (it stores at index 0 and the pending-error check\nafter it leaves), so a call sits on a path back to the latch and the\nheader's length load (and data pointer) is reloaded every iteration.\nThe get's failing arm leaves the loop after C-error-flag-fold; the\nset's does not.\n\nWhat would let the versioning fire: the store's failing arm leaving the\nloop like the get's (error-flag / P-list-access-and-loops), and the\nwrite summaries / typed alias rules (C-write-summaries,\nC-licm-typed-alias) that let LICM hoist a header load past calls and\nstores that cannot touch it. Nothing in bounds_version needs to change\nfor those shapes once the length is read before the loop.","files":null}]}